  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 88f6fa2b89436164b8e97b17d82dc9a2, type: 3}
  m_Name: 6 - Variables
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: UtilityScriptIWrite::HintSO
  hintId: tutorial_variables
  isUnlockedByDefault: 0
  ignoreLock: 1
  title: Variables
  image: {fileID: 0}
  description: 'A variable is a named storage used in a program to hold a value.

    Variables
    allow you to store data such as numbers, text, or boolean values and reuse them
    later in your program.


    Rules for naming variables:

    <indent=2em>- Must
    start with a letter or underscore ( _ )</indent>

    <indent=2em>- Cannot start
    with a number</indent>

    <indent=2em>- Can only contain letters, numbers,
    and underscores</indent>

    <indent=2em>- No spaces allowed</indent>

    <indent=2em>-
    Should not use reserved keywords</indent>


    Correct naming examples:

    <font="JetBrainsMono
    SDF"><color=#7FDFFF>energy_level</color>, <color=#7FDFFF>currentTile</color>,
    <color=#7FDFFF>is_blocked</color></font>


    Wrong naming examples:

    <font="JetBrainsMono
    SDF"><color=#FF6B6B>1energy</color></font> (starts with a number),

    <font="JetBrainsMono
    SDF"><color=#FF6B6B>current tile</color></font> (contains space),

    <font="JetBrainsMono
    SDF"><color=#FF6B6B>class</color></font> (reserved keyword)


    The <font="JetBrainsMono
    SDF"><color=#7FDFFF>=</color></font> symbol is called assignment.

    It is
    used to store a value inside a variable.


    Example:

    <font="JetBrainsMono
    SDF"><color=#7FDFFF>energy</color> = 10</font>

    <font="JetBrainsMono SDF"><color=#7FDFFF>tile</color>
    = "wall"</font>

    <font="JetBrainsMono SDF"><color=#7FDFFF>isBlocked</color>
    = True</font>'
  isImageBig: 0
